Link Qt6Qml against the SDK's -lkandelo-mprotect on Kandelo.

masm/wtf/OSAllocatorPosix.cpp commits V4 JS heap pages with
mprotect(PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE). Kandelo's libc.a leaves mprotect()
out; -lkandelo-mprotect carries out requests that keep read and write
access. As a private library of a static Qt6Qml it lands in
Qt6QmlTargets.cmake as $<LINK_ONLY:kandelo-mprotect>, so every CMake
consumer links it without naming it.

The same file's decommit() asks for PROT_NONE after MADV_DONTNEED and
calls CRASH() if refused. -lkandelo-mprotect refuses it with ENOTSUP, so
a program that links Qt6Qml declares the mprotect fiction for that
call in its own package.toml (quickshell does). Library packages cannot
declare fictions.

--- a/src/qml/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/src/qml/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -758,6 +758,18 @@
     SOURCES
         ../3rdparty/masm/wtf/OSAllocatorPosix.cpp
 )
+
+# Kandelo (wasm32posix): ../3rdparty/masm/wtf/OSAllocatorPosix.cpp commits JS
+# heap pages with mprotect(PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE), and Kandelo's libc.a
+# leaves mprotect() out. The SDK's -lkandelo-mprotect carries out such
+# requests; a private library of a static Qt6Qml lands in its exported link
+# interface, so every consumer links it without naming it. The same file's
+# decommit() also asks for PROT_NONE, which that library refuses with
+# ENOTSUP and decommit() answers with CRASH(): a program linking Qt6Qml
+# declares the mprotect fiction for it (see quickshell's package.toml).
+if(LINUX AND CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR MATCHES "^wasm(32|64)$")
+    qt_internal_extend_target(Qml LIBRARIES kandelo-mprotect)
+endif()
 
 qt_internal_extend_target(Qml CONDITION DEFINES___contains___WTF_USE_UDIS86=1
     SOURCES
